In the studio the code runs in Node, so the versions come from `process`:

```ts
console.log('node ' + process.version);
console.log('v8   ' + process.versions.v8);
// node v20.11.0
// v8   11.3.244.8-node.17
```

npm is a separate binary rather than part of the runtime, so it has no `process.versions` entry — read it from your own terminal with `npm -v` and note it in a comment.

**What good looks like:** two real numbers off *your* machine. The reason the lesson asks is that "works on my machine" is a version story more often than a code story — when a colleague's scaffold behaves differently, the first question is which Node they are on.

> Playwright needs Node 18+; the course was built on Node 20. If yours is older, that is worth fixing before Week 3.
